# {{ name }}: guide for coding agents

This is a Solarian robot project, written against Solarian {{ version }}. This file
is regenerated by `solarian agent sync`; do not edit it. Notes people wrote for
agents are in `{{ notes }}`. Read them too.

## Before you change anything

- Read the docs of the installed version rather than relying on memory: `solarian docs list`, `solarian docs show <page>`, `solarian docs search <term>`.
- Let the CLI do what it owns. Add hardware or a published skill with `solarian install <plugin>`, which also seeds its config into `conf/`. Put code meant for other projects in a plugin (`solarian create-plugin`). `solarian <command> --help` describes any command.
- Follow the skill for the task; they are listed at the end of this file.

## Architecture

Three processes, each of which can run on its own machine:

- **Robot** (`{{ package }}/robot.py`, a `solarian.core.Robot`) owns the hardware. Its class attributes are **modules** (an arm, a gripper, a camera), each made of actuators and sensors and run in its own process at `frequency` Hz.
- **Relay server** (`{{ package }}/server.py`, a `solarian.core.RelayServer`) forwards actions to the robot and observations back.
- **Controller** (`{{ package }}/controller.py`, a `solarian.core.Controller`) decides what happens: teleop, skills, and the agentic controller, which plans with a vision-language model and calls skills as tools.

An `Action` (`action.py`) goes to the robot and an `Observation` (`observation.py`) comes back. Both are Pydantic schemas with one entry per module. **Skills** (`solarian.core.skill.Skill`) run at their own rate, read observations and return actions; learned policies run as **services** that skills call over HTTP. `{{ package }}/settings.py` names the three classes for the CLI.

## Project map

```text
{{ name }}/
├── {{ package }}/
│   ├── settings.py        # ROBOT_CLASS, CONTROLLER_CLASS, SERVER_CLASS
│   ├── robot.py           # one class attribute per module, and the sim scene
│   ├── modules/           # local Module classes
│   ├── observation.py     # one ModuleObs per module, SensorField / CalculatedField
│   ├── action.py          # one ModuleAction per module, ActuatorField
│   ├── controller.py      # tools the agent may call, key handlers, on_start
│   ├── server.py
│   ├── skills/            # Skill classes
│   └── datasets/          # dataset ops, one DatasetOp per file
├── conf/
│   ├── robot.yaml  controller.yaml  server.yaml
│   ├── modules/<module>.yaml      # one per robot attribute, same name
│   ├── skills/<skill>.yaml
│   ├── services/<service>.yaml
│   ├── commands/record.yaml
│   ├── training/<run>.yaml
│   └── deployment/<name>.yaml     # where things run: IPs, ports, device paths, URLs
├── recordings/  models/           # data; not in git
└── {{ notes }}
```

## Invariants

- **One name per module.** The robot attribute (`arm = MyArmModule()`), `conf/modules/arm.yaml`, the attribute on the `Observation` and `Action` subclasses, and the key in a skill's `observations` and `action_queues` are the same string. A module without its `ModuleObs` and `ModuleAction` fails at startup.
- **A skill never holds a Module.** It reads `self.observations[name]` and returns `Action(action_type=ActionType.MODULE_ACTION, data={name: {...}})`, with module names passed in `params` (`{"arm": "arm", "gripper": "gripper"}`). That is what lets a skill run on the controller, the robot or the server. Modules interpret actions; they get no methods that build actions for others.
- **On the controller, a skill gets action queues only for `params["arm"]` and `params["gripper"]`.** Actions for other modules are dropped with a warning.
- **Units.** Arm joints are normalized to [-100, 100] in observations and actions (radians only inside the module). Cartesian `x, y, z` are metres, and `rx, ry, rz` are a rotation vector in radians. A gripper is commanded in millimetres of aperture but observed as its servo position, normalized to [-100, 100] (-100 closed). `moveJ` speeds are rad/s and `moveL` speeds m/s.
- **Config belongs to whoever owns the concept.** Named poses and task vocabulary go in the skill's `conf/skills/<skill>.yaml`; ends of travel and speed limits go in the module's yaml.
- **Anything that depends on where a process runs** (IPs, ports, device paths, server URLs, checkpoints, tokens) lives only in `conf/deployment/<name>.yaml`, read elsewhere as `${deployment.<key>}`. Pick a profile with `-d <name>` or `SOLARIAN_DEPLOYMENT`; `localhost` is the default.
- **The agentic controller sees a skill only if** the controller lists it in `tools` as `Tool(SkillClass, {bound params}, agent_enabled=True)` and the class sets a `description` attribute. Docstrings are not read.
- **Simulation first.** Anything that moves runs in `--sim` before it runs on hardware, and a first run on hardware needs the user's go-ahead.

## Check a change

```bash
python -c "import {{ package }}.robot, {{ package }}.observation, {{ package }}.action, {{ package }}.controller"
solarian run-server                  # terminal 1
solarian run-robot --sim --view      # terminal 2; needs pip install "solarian[sim]"
solarian run-controller              # terminal 3
pytest                               # when tests/ exists
```

`run-server`, `run-robot` and `run-controller` run until stopped: start them in the background, read their output, and stop them when done. Leave out `--sim` or pass `-d <hardware profile>` only when the user asks for the real robot.

